invariant: INV-26
file: ghash_hw.c
catches: ghash_equiv_test
reason: the AES=hw multiply puts the high half of the cross products' sum
  where its low half belongs and the reverse, so the unreduced product is
  wrong in its middle two words whenever the cross products are nonzero.
  It is the mistake a four-product schoolbook multiply invites, and it
  leaves right every product whose cross products are zero, such as two
  operands whose low halves are both zero, which is why
  bin/ghash_equiv_test runs every pair of single-bit operands and random
  pairs beside the fixed ones. The edit is shared C
  after either instruction, so it lands on an ARMv8 runner and an x86-64
  one alike.

--- old
    uint64_t word1 = product_high(s->sums.low) ^ product_low(s->sums.cross);
    uint64_t word2 = product_low(s->sums.high) ^ product_high(s->sums.cross);
--- new
    uint64_t word1 = product_high(s->sums.low) ^ product_high(s->sums.cross);
    uint64_t word2 = product_low(s->sums.high) ^ product_low(s->sums.cross);
